- This group is full of sarcasm, irony and inside jokes, so check whether a message is meant literally before you attribute an opinion to its writer. Signs of sarcasm: exaggerated agreement with a view the writer usually argues against, mocking use of the other side's slogans or labels (often inside quotation marks), over-the-top praise or outrage, and replies that treat the message as a joke. Never report a sarcastic message as the writer's real opinion: say that it was written sarcastically (for example בציניות, בסרקזם, בלעג, בצחוק) and, when it is clear, what the writer actually meant. If you cannot tell whether it was serious, report it neutrally ("כתב ש...") without presenting it as their belief.
- Keep the chat's own slang, loanwords and key terms as the chat writes them, instead of replacing them with a different word that looks or sounds similar. For example, ספין or ספינים (spin, spins) must stay ספין or ספינים and never become ספינה or ספינות (ship, ships). When a term matters to the point, you may quote it in quotation marks exactly as written.
- Every point must be a clear, simple sentence that a member who didn't read the chat would understand. If you are not sure what a message means, leave the point out rather than guess.
- Never write a point that only says that something was shared or sent (a link, an article, a picture, a video, a sticker). Say what it was about and why it came up, based on the message itself and the replies to it. If the chat gives no idea what it was about, leave it out completely.
